.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt RZONE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Zone
.Nd local time for raz programs: offsets, abbreviations and dates
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
import Zone;

type Time = record
    year, month, day: INT;
    hour, minute, second: INT;
    weekday: INT;
    yearday: INT;
    offset: INT;
    abbr: []U8;
end;

proc local(t: I64): Time;
proc offset(t: I64): (INT, []U8);
proc split(t: I64; off: INT): Time;
proc days(y, m, d: INT): I64;
proc date(n: I64): (INT, INT, INT);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a raz module in rzone, a repository a program adds as a submodule
and builds with
.Fl I Pa rzone/src .
It answers what C's
.Fn localtime_r
answers: given an instant, in seconds since 1970-01-01 UTC, as
.Li Sys.now() div 1000000000
gives it, how far the local clock is ahead of UTC then, what the zone
calls that clock, and the date and time the clock shows.
.Pp
.Fn local
gives all of it, as a
.Vt Time :
.Fa month
from 1 to 12,
.Fa day
from 1 to 31,
.Fa hour ,
.Fa minute
and
.Fa second
as a clock shows them,
.Fa weekday
from 0 for Sunday to 6 for Saturday,
.Fa yearday
from 0 for 1 January to 365,
.Fa offset
in seconds east of UTC, and
.Fa abbr ,
the zone's name for the clock:
.Ql BST ,
.Ql EDT ,
.Ql +0545 .
The abbreviation is kept by the module for as long as the program
runs.
.Fn offset
gives only the offset and the abbreviation, for a program that writes
times as UTC and an offset, or wants no more.
.Pp
.Fn split
gives the
.Vt Time
of
.Fa t
on a clock a fixed
.Fa off
seconds east of UTC, its
.Fa abbr
empty: UTC itself with an
.Fa off
of 0, or a time kept with the offset it was written in.
.Pp
.Fn days
gives the day of
.Fa y Ns - Ns Fa m Ns - Ns Fa d ,
a number that is 0 for 1970-01-01 and counts on from it, and back
before it below 0, for a month from 1 to 12; a day past the end of
its month runs on into the next, so that
.Li days(2026, 1, 32)
is 1 February.
.Fn date
gives the year, month and day of day
.Fa n .
Both follow the proleptic Gregorian calendar, by Howard Hinnant's
algorithms, for any year an
.Vt INT
holds; with them, a date steps by whole days and two dates subtract.
.Ss The zone
The zone is the one
.Ev TZ
names, read as the GNU C library reads it:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ev TZ
unset: the file
.Pa /etc/localtime .
.It
.Ev TZ
empty: UTC.
.It
Otherwise, a leading
.Ql \&:
dropped, the file it names: a path if it starts with
.Ql / ,
else a name under
.Ev TZDIR ,
or
.Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo
if
.Ev TZDIR
is unset or empty, such as
.Ql Europe/London .
.It
If there is no such file,
.Ev TZ
itself as a POSIX rule, such as
.Ql EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0
or
.Ql <+1030>-10:30<+11>-11,M10.1.0,M4.1.0 :
a standard clock's name and its offset west of UTC, and maybe a
summer clock's name, its offset, an hour ahead if it gives none, and
the date and time each begins.
A rule with a summer clock and no dates takes the United States'
since 2007,
.Ql M3.2.0,M11.1.0 .
.It
Anything else, a file that isn't TZif or a rule that doesn't read, is
UTC, with the abbreviation
.Ql UTC .
.El
.Pp
A zone file is TZif (RFC 8536), version 1 or later: the instants at
which the clock changed, and the clock from each on; before the first,
the file's first clock. From version 2 the file ends in a POSIX rule
for the instants after the last change, which
.Nm
follows as it follows one in
.Ev TZ .
.Pp
The zone is read once, at the first call of
.Fn local
or
.Fn offset ,
and kept; each answer after that is a search of the table, or a few
days worked out by the rule. As with C's
.Fn localtime_r ,
a change to
.Ev TZ
or to the file after that isn't seen.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The time now, and the zone's name for the clock:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tm := Zone.local(Sys.now() div 1000000000);
Fmt.print("{}-{}-{} {}:{} {}\en", [Fmt.int(tm.year), Fmt.int(tm.month), Fmt.int(tm.day),
    Fmt.int(tm.hour), Fmt.int(tm.minute), Fmt.str(tm.abbr)]);
.Ed
.Pp
Today, as a day number, and the day a week from it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tm := Zone.local(Sys.now() div 1000000000);
today := Zone.days(tm.year, tm.month, tm.day);
y, m, d := Zone.date(today + 7);
.Ed
.Pp
An instant from a local date and time, at the offset the zone had a
day before; the offset of the instant itself may differ near a change
of the clock:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t := Zone.days(y, m, d) * 86400 + h * 3600 + min * 60;
off, _ := Zone.offset(t - 86400);
t := t - off;
.Ed
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 8536, the Time Zone Information Format; POSIX's
.Ev TZ ,
in the Base Definitions, section 8.3.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr localtime 3 ,
.Xr tzfile 5 ,
.Xr zic 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Where the C library is wrong,
.Nm
doesn't follow it.
For a zone that is only a rule, the C library works out each year
before 1970 as if it were 1970, so that its summer comes at the wrong
instants;
.Nm
works the year out.
A rule whose summer is the whole year, such as
.Ql XXX3YYY,0/0,J365/25 ,
is summer throughout here, as zic means it; the C library gives a few
hours of standard time at each new year.
And a name that is no file and no rule, such as
.Ql Bogus/Name ,
is UTC here, where the C library keeps the letters it starts with as
the abbreviation,
.Ql Bogus .
.Pp
Left out: the zones that count leap seconds, the
.Pa right/
ones, which are UTC here; the C library's
.Pa posixrules
file, for a rule with no dates; local time back to an instant, which
is ambiguous where the clock goes back and missing where it goes
forward; and formatting.
The first call isn't safe from two threads at once: a program with
threads asks once before it starts them.
